<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Trithemius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Trithemius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Trithemius was a German Benedictine abbot, humanist and scholar, abbot of Sponheim from 1483 and later of St James at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>. He built Sponheim's library into one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and wrote histories, catalogues of authors and the Steganographia and Polygraphia, early works on cryptography. He taught and corresponded with figures such as Cornelius Agrippa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-trithemius.jpg" alt="Johannes Trithemius" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CSvBibra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trithemiusmoredetail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johannes Trithemius was born Johann Heidenberg, and his byname refers to his native town of Trittenheim on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, then part of the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. His father died while he was an infant. His stepfather, whom his mother married seven years later, was hostile to education, so the boy learned in secret and with difficulty, acquiring Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. At seventeen he left home to look for good teachers and studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">University of Heidelberg</a>. In 1482 a snowstorm drove him to take refuge in the Benedictine abbey of Sponheim near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-kreuznach">Bad Kreuznach</a>, and he decided to stay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected abbot at the age of 21, Trithemius set out to turn a neglected and undisciplined house into a centre of learning. During his time the library grew from around fifty items to more than two thousand. Differences with his convent, and a reputation as a magician that did not help him, led to his resignation in 1506. He accepted an offer from Lorenz von Bibra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, to become abbot of St James's Abbey, the Schottenkloster in Würzburg, where he remained until his death.</p><p>He wrote chronicles of Sponheim and a two-volume history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirsau-abbey">Hirsau Abbey</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> was admired, but critics even in his lifetime pointed out that he had invented sources, such as a Scythian historian called Hunibald, and a chronicler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> called Meginfrid. Among his pupils were Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paracelsus">Paracelsus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known work, the Steganographia, was written around 1499 and printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1606. It appears to be about using spirits to communicate over long distances, but after the decryption key to the first two volumes was published in 1606 they were seen to concern cryptography and steganography. The work was placed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum">Index Librorum Prohibitorum</a> in 1609 and removed in 1900. His Polygraphia was written in 1508 and published in 1518.</p><p>Trithemius also had a reputation as a necromancer. In 1507 he became the first author to mention the historical Johann Faust of Knittlingen, in a letter that criticised Faust's vanity, and later tellings tied the Faust legend to stories of him conjuring the shade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> for Maximilian I. He was buried in the church of St James's Abbey, with a tombstone by Tilman Riemenschneider, which was later moved to the Neumünster church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johannes Trithemius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The abbey of Sponheim later had the humanist <b>Johannes Trithemius</b> as abbot from 1483.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trittenheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.83,6.9&amp;year=1497" aria-label="Trittenheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johannes Trithemius"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 5846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 5846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.83,6.9&amp;year=1497">What was here in 1497? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
